{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs36 Motion Error\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The actual axis position (encoder position) may differ from the controller commanded position (instantaneous output of the profile generator) during motion or at rest. The difference between the commanded position and actual position is commonly referred to as the position error, motion error, or following error. When the position error exceeds a maximum allowable value (typically used to prevent safety issues, motor overheating, or equipment damage), the motion controller can put the axis into a safe operating state. This is referred to as a motion error.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To determine whether a motion error has occurred, the position error limit is continuously compared against the actual position error. If the position error limit value is exceeded, then the axis reports a motion error.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When a motion error is detected, one of the following actions can be automatically performed:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b None} - No action is taken by the controller. {\i This is not recommended.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b AbruptStop} - The axis is brought to an abrupt stop (commanded position set to actual position at time of motion error).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b SmoothStop} - The axis is brought to a smooth stop using the current deceleration setting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b DisableMotor} - The axis motor is disabled (de-energized). Note, this can cause a runaway condition and drive the axis into its hard stop.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There are several ways to recover from a motion error:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab Perform a {\i Reset Position}. If the motor has been disabled (de-energized), the motor must be {\i Re-enabled} (this is done automatically by the GUI).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab Perform an {\i Initialize} followed by a {\i Home} of the individual axis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab Perform an {\i Initialize and Home All} of all axes.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The motion position error limit settings are stored in the motion controller's NVRAM so they persist across power cycles. After changing the Position Error limit settings, the axis must be de-energized before they can be written to NVRAM. i.e.\par}
